/// \defgroup grp_w1020_ww3d_render_object_helpers WW3D2 render-object helpers: lights, scene fog, texture release, line renderer, bounds, mesh normals, water grid, sorting list and asset lookup
/// \ingroup grp_w3d_rendering
/// \brief Small members of the W3D engine's render objects and scenes: light
///        colour setters, the scene fog range, the texture reference release,
///        the default object-space bounding box, the ring object's LOD cost,
///        the mesh vertex-normal test, the water grid transform, sorting-list
///        insertion and the asset-registry existence check.
///
/// Their code belongs to the WW3D2 library and the W3D device layer
/// (\ref sub_w3d_rendering describes both). Several of them are header
/// inlines of which retail keeps a single out-of-line copy. To see where the
/// project currently keeps each body, search reverse/functions.csv for the
/// function's name with rg. They sit at different points of the W3D
/// pipeline:
///
/// - Assets. Render_Obj_Exists asks BFME 2's asset registry whether a name
///   is registered, before an asset is registered again or loaded.
/// - Render objects. RenderObjClass::Get_Obj_Space_Bounding_Box is the base
///   class's empty bounding box; RingRenderObjClass::Get_Cost reports a ring
///   object's cost to the level-of-detail manager.
/// - Meshes. MeshModelClass::Needs_Vertex_Normals decides whether a mesh's
///   vertex buffer carries normals.
/// - Materials. TextureBaseClass::Release_Ref and TextureClass::Release_Ref
///   drop one reference to a texture; SegLineRendererClass (the line
///   renderer behind segmented lines, streaks and line-mode particles) shares
///   its texture that way.
/// - Scenes and lights. LightClass::Set_Ambient, LightClass::Set_Diffuse and
///   LightClass::Set_Specular set a light's colours;
///   SceneClass::Get_Fog_Range reads a scene's fog distances.
/// - Transparent sorting. DLNodeClass::Insert_Before links a node into a
///   doubly linked list; the sorting renderer uses it to keep deferred
///   transparent geometry in depth order.
/// - Device layer. WaterRenderObjClass::setGridTransform positions the water
///   grid.
///
/// \ref grp_w2_ww3d_core_helpers holds the neighbouring WW3D2 helpers,
/// including SegLineRendererClass::operator=, MeshModelClass::Compute_Ram_Size
/// and Register_Aggregate_Prototype.
///
/// \par Differences from Zero Hour
/// Established by comparing the BFME 2 bodies with Zero Hour's source:
/// - Texture reference counts are 16 bits wide and share their word with
///   flag bits. Releasing a texture whose count is already zero does
///   nothing, and reaching zero deletes the texture only when one flag bit is
///   set (TextureBaseClass::Release_Ref). Zero Hour's RefCountClass keeps a
///   full integer count and always deletes at zero.
/// - WaterRenderObjClass::setGridTransform hands the matrix to a separate
///   grid object. Zero Hour's and the BFME 1 reconstruction's version apply
///   it to the water render object itself.
/// - SegLineRendererClass has one extra field, as the BFME 1 reconstruction
///   also found; see the class page.
///
/// The light setters, the fog range getter, the default bounding box, the
/// ring cost, the vertex-normal test and the list insertion are Zero Hour's
/// code unchanged.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Asset names are looked up case-insensitively. The registry turns the
///   name into a lower-case name key before the lookup, so for
///   Render_Obj_Exists names that differ only in case are the same asset.
/// - Optional companion assets are found by name. For example, when a
///   global-data flag is set (its INI field is not identified),
///   RoadType::loadTexture derives a companion normal-map texture name from
///   the road texture's name and loads that texture only if
///   Render_Obj_Exists finds the name. A missing companion is skipped
///   silently.
/// - For line-mode particle emitters, the line settings are W3D data:
///   ParticleBufferClass's constructor passes the emitter's line properties
///   to SegLineRendererClass::Init (see the class page for which fields come
///   from there). Other owners, such as the segmented-line objects used by
///   draw modules like W3DLaserDraw, set texture, width, scroll rate and
///   tiling in code (Zero Hour: from W3DLaserDraw's INI fields).
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - DX8Wrapper::Set_Light is the fixed-function path: it turns a LightClass
///   into a Direct3D light and scales the ambient, diffuse and specular
///   colours by the light's intensity. BFME 2 also passes ambient,
///   directional and point lights to its FX effect shaders
///   (FXShaderParameterSourceNamespaceSAS). A port has to cover both paths.
/// - Vertex normals can be required by the device, not only by the mesh.
///   MeshMatDescClass::Do_Mappers_Need_Normals returns true whenever the
///   Direct3D device supports N-patches and the N-patch level is above one,
///   and MeshModelClass::Needs_Vertex_Normals passes that answer on. N-patch
///   tessellation is a Direct3D 8/9 feature; a port to another API drops or
///   emulates it, and should check that meshes still get normals when they
///   need them for lighting.
/// - Texture reference counts are 16 bits wide, and some callers increment
///   them inline rather than through a function; see
///   TextureBaseClass::Release_Ref before changing them.


/// \file GameClient/Render_Obj_Exists.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_ww3d_render_object_helpers
/// \brief Render_Obj_Exists, the free function that asks the asset registry
///        whether a name is registered.
///
/// Ported from the Open-BFME-1 unit of the same name. The registry's lookup
/// itself is AssetRegistry::Render_Obj_Exists_Impl.


/// \class LightClass
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_ww3d_render_object_helpers
/// \brief A W3D light: a point, directional or spot light with ambient,
///        diffuse and specular colours, an intensity, attenuation ranges and
///        spot-light settings.
///
/// Zero Hour: LightClass is a render object whose Render does nothing. When
/// a light is added to a scene, LightClass::Notify_Added registers it with
/// the scene as a light, and LightClass::Notify_Removed unregisters it; both
/// BFME 2 bodies match Zero Hour's. DX8Wrapper::Set_Light converts a light
/// into a Direct3D fixed-function light: it multiplies the diffuse, specular
/// and ambient colours by the intensity and passes the position, spot
/// direction, attenuation range, spot exponent and spot angle along. The
/// display creates the game's global directional lights at start-up
/// (\ref sub_w3d_rendering_entry).
///
/// \par Defaults
/// BFME 2's constructor, like Zero Hour's, makes a light of the requested
/// type (Zero Hour's header defaults it to point) with intensity 1.0, white
/// ambient, diffuse and specular colours, near attenuation 0 to 0, far
/// attenuation 50 to 100, a 45-degree spot angle, spot exponent 1.0 and
/// spot direction (0, 0, 1). A directional light is marked force-visible,
/// because it has no position that culling could test. The copy
/// constructor copies every light setting; the render-object base is
/// default-constructed, not copied.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// A light is reference counted like every render object, and LightClass::Clone
/// returns a new light made by the copy constructor.
///
/// \par Bounds
/// A light's object-space bounding sphere and box are centred on the light
/// and reach out to the end of its far attenuation range
/// (LightClass::Get_Obj_Space_Bounding_Sphere,
/// LightClass::Get_Obj_Space_Bounding_Box), the same distance
/// DX8Wrapper::Set_Light gives Direct3D as the light's range.
///
/// \par BFME 2 notes
/// The colour setters and most getters are header inlines. Retail keeps
/// out-of-line copies of LightClass::Set_Ambient, LightClass::Set_Diffuse and
/// LightClass::Set_Specular. The BFME 1 reconstruction found that its
/// retail LightClass virtual table does not give the persistence functions
/// (Get_Factory, Save, Load) slots as Zero Hour's header does; whether BFME
/// 2's table agrees is not established here.
///
/// \see LightClass::Set_Ambient, LightClass::Set_Diffuse,
///      LightClass::Set_Specular, SceneClass::Get_Fog_Range,
///      \ref grp_w3d_rendering


/// \fn void LightClass::Set_Ambient(const Vector3 &color)
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_ww3d_render_object_helpers
/// \brief Sets the light's ambient colour.
///
/// The header inline from light.h, which stores the colour unchanged; retail
/// keeps one out-of-line copy, and its callers are not identified. The
/// colour is not scaled here. DX8Wrapper::Set_Light multiplies it by the
/// light's intensity when it builds the Direct3D light.
///
/// \param color Ambient colour as red, green and blue, nominally 0 to 1.
///
/// \see LightClass::Set_Diffuse, LightClass::Set_Specular,
///      LightClass::Get_Ambient


/// \fn void LightClass::Set_Diffuse(const Vector3 &color)
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_ww3d_render_object_helpers
/// \brief Sets the light's diffuse colour.
///
/// The header inline from light.h, which stores the colour unchanged; retail
/// keeps one out-of-line copy, and its callers are not identified.
/// DX8Wrapper::Set_Light multiplies the colour by the light's intensity
/// when it builds the Direct3D light.
///
/// \param color Diffuse colour as red, green and blue, nominally 0 to 1.
///
/// \see LightClass::Set_Ambient, LightClass::Set_Specular,
///      LightClass::Get_Diffuse


/// \fn void LightClass::Set_Specular(const Vector3 &color)
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_ww3d_render_object_helpers
/// \brief Sets the light's specular colour.
///
/// The header inline from light.h, which stores the colour unchanged; retail
/// keeps one out-of-line copy, and its callers are not identified.
/// DX8Wrapper::Set_Light multiplies the colour by the light's intensity
/// when it builds the Direct3D light.
///
/// \param color Specular colour as red, green and blue, nominally 0 to 1.
///
/// \see LightClass::Set_Ambient, LightClass::Set_Diffuse,
///      LightClass::Get_Specular


/// \fn void SceneClass::Get_Fog_Range(float *start, float *end)
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_ww3d_render_object_helpers
/// \brief Returns the distances at which the scene's fog starts and reaches
///        full strength.
///
/// A virtual function of SceneClass, the base of the game's scenes. It is the
/// header inline from scene.h, of which retail keeps one out-of-line copy.
/// Callers reach it through the scene's virtual table, and
/// no direct caller is identified. Zero Hour: SceneClass::Set_Fog_Range sets
/// the two distances, a new scene starts with fog disabled, black, from 0 to
/// 1000, and SceneClass::Render passes the fog settings to DX8Wrapper::Set_Fog.
///
/// \param start [out] Receives the fog start distance. Must not be null.
/// \param end   [out] Receives the fog end distance. Must not be null.
///
/// \see LightClass, \ref sub_w3d_rendering


/// \fn void TextureBaseClass::Release_Ref(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_ww3d_render_object_helpers
/// \brief Drops one reference to a texture and, when the count reaches zero
///        and a flag bit is set, deletes the texture.
///
/// The release half of texture sharing. Code that keeps a texture pointer
/// adds a reference when it takes the pointer and calls this when it lets
/// go. Its callers include a material pass
/// (W3DShroudMaterialPassClass::Install_Materials), the line renderer
/// (SegLineRendererClass::operator=), terrain, water and radar code
/// (BaseHeightMapRenderObjClass::initHeightData,
/// WaterRenderObjClass::loadSetting, W3DRadar::buildTerrainTexture) and the
/// texture smart pointer's assignment, RefCountPtr::operator=. It has
/// hundreds of call sites; tools/callers_of.py lists them.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The reference count is the low 16 bits of the word that follows the
/// virtual table pointer; higher bits of the same word hold flags.
/// - If the count is already zero, the call does nothing.
/// - Otherwise the count is decremented.
/// - If it has reached zero and a flag (bit 24 of the word) is set, the
///   texture's virtual Delete_This is called, which destroys it.
///
/// Taking a reference increments the same 16-bit count. Some callers inline
/// the increment (SegLineRendererClass::operator= does); an out-of-line
/// TextureBaseClass::Add_Ref also exists.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's textures inherit RefCountClass, whose count is a full
///       integer and whose release always deletes at zero (and asserts in
///       debug builds if the count goes negative). BFME 2's guard against
///       releasing at zero and the flag bit that gates deletion have no Zero
///       Hour counterpart. What sets or clears the flag, and what happens to a
///       texture that reaches zero without it, is not established.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// A count of 16 bits wraps after 65,535 references. Some callers increment
/// it inline (SegLineRendererClass::operator= is one), so raising the limit
/// changes the texture layout those callers are compiled against, not only
/// this function.
///
/// \see TextureClass::Release_Ref, SegLineRendererClass,
///      \ref grp_w2_ww3d_core_helpers


/// \fn void TextureClass::Release_Ref(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_ww3d_render_object_helpers
/// \brief The texture release as TextureClass callers name it; the same
///        retail body as TextureBaseClass::Release_Ref.
///
/// This name and TextureBaseClass::Release_Ref denote the same retail body.
/// The ledger records TextureClass::Release_Ref as an alias of it, because
/// callers that release a TextureClass reach that body; the name comes from
/// the Open-BFME-1 reconstruction. Whether retail compiled a separate
/// TextureClass function that was folded, or TextureClass callers reach the
/// inherited base member, is not established.
///
/// \see TextureBaseClass::Release_Ref


/// \class SegLineRendererClass
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_ww3d_render_object_helpers
/// \brief The low-level line renderer: draws a polyline as a textured strip
///        of quads, with subdivision noise, intersection merging and
///        scrolling texture coordinates.
///
/// Zero Hour's header describes it as the line rendering shared by
/// SegmentedLineClass and ParticleBufferClass. In BFME 2 the constructors of
/// SegmentedLineClass, StreakLineClass and ParticleBufferClass create one, and
/// the assignment operators of SegmentedLineClass and StreakLineClass copy it
/// with SegLineRendererClass::operator=. The renderer holds only appearance
/// settings and a scratch vertex buffer (Zero Hour: the owner passes the
/// points and its transform to Render each frame).
///
/// \par Settings
/// A texture, a shader, a width, a colour and an opacity; a subdivision
/// level and noise amplitude (random offsets added to subdivided points); a
/// merge-abort factor for intersection merging; a texture tile factor and
/// mapping mode (uniform width, uniform length or tiled); a UV offset and a
/// UV scroll rate; and flag bits for merging intersections, freezing the
/// random offsets, disabling sorting and drawing end caps. BFME 2's
/// constructor sets the defaults: the preset additive sprite shader, width
/// 0, white, opacity 1.0, subdivision level 0, noise 0, merge-abort factor
/// 1.5, tile factor 1.0, the current sync time as the scroll start, no UV
/// offset or scroll, and intersection merging on with uniform-width mapping.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// - The texture is shared by reference count: copying a renderer adds a
///   reference to the incoming texture before releasing the old one (Zero
///   Hour's Set_Texture does the same), and the destructor releases it
///   (TextureBaseClass::Release_Ref).
/// - The vertex buffer belongs to the renderer and is never copied. It grows
///   on demand to one and a half times the requested size, and the
///   destructor frees it. BFME 2's destructor frees the buffer before
///   releasing the texture; Zero Hour's does the reverse, which makes no
///   difference to callers.
///
/// \par BFME 2 layout
/// The class has one more field than Zero Hour's, placed after the opacity.
/// Its role is unknown. The BFME 2 bodies show that the constructor sets it to
/// zero, SegLineRendererClass::operator= copies it, and
/// SegLineRendererClass::Scale multiplies it by the scale factor together
/// with the width and the noise amplitude (Zero Hour's Scale scales only
/// those two), which suggests a length; ParticleBufferClass::Scale calls
/// that Scale. The BFME 1 reconstruction found the same extra field.
///
/// \par Modding
/// For line-mode particle emitters the settings are W3D data.
/// ParticleBufferClass's constructor passes the emitter's line properties
/// (a W3dEmitterLinePropertiesStruct) to SegLineRendererClass::Init, which
/// reads from them the four flags, the texture mapping mode, the subdivision
/// level, the noise amplitude, the merge-abort factor, the texture tile
/// factor and the U and V scroll speeds, given per second and stored per
/// millisecond. SegLineRendererClass::Set_Texture_Tile_Factor clamps the
/// tile factor to the range 0 to 50. Width, colour, opacity and texture are
/// set by the owning object rather than read here.
///
/// \see SegLineRendererClass::operator=, SegLineRendererClass::Init,
///      SegLineRendererClass::Scale, TextureBaseClass::Release_Ref,
///      \ref grp_w2_ww3d_core_helpers


/// \fn void RenderObjClass::Get_Obj_Space_Bounding_Box(AABoxClass &box) const
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_ww3d_render_object_helpers
/// \brief The base render object's bounding box: a box of zero size at the
///        object's origin.
///
/// Every render object can report an axis-aligned bounding box in its own
/// coordinate space; derived classes such as meshes and lights override this
/// virtual function with their real extents.
/// This base version, which matches Zero Hour's, sets both the box's centre
/// and its extent to zero. Zero Hour: the cached world-space bounding volumes
/// that culling tests are computed from the object-space ones.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Retail folded this body with ParticleEmitterClass's identical override,
/// so the RenderObjClass and ParticleEmitterClass virtual tables both point
/// at the one copy. ParticleEmitterClass reports an empty box in Zero Hour
/// too.
///
/// \param box [out] Receives the zero box.
///
/// \see RingRenderObjClass, LightClass, \ref sub_w3d_rendering


/// \class RingRenderObjClass
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_ww3d_render_object_helpers
/// \brief A ring (flat annulus) render object, an animatable primitive used
///        for effects.
///
/// Zero Hour: a RingRenderObjClass draws a camera-aligned or fixed ring
/// between an inner and an outer extent, with a texture, shader and texture
/// tiling. Its colour, opacity and inner and outer scales can be animated
/// over a set duration by linear-interpolation channels, and the ring is
/// defined by a W3dRingStruct in W3D data and created through a prototype
/// (RingPrototypeClass). It supports level of detail: a fixed set of shared
/// ring meshes of increasing polygon count, chosen through the
/// RenderObjClass LOD interface (Prepare_LOD, Increment_LOD, Get_Cost,
/// Get_Value and related functions).
///
/// The BFME 1 reconstruction's ringobj.cpp is the reference source for BFME
/// 2's copy. The description above is Zero Hour's; how much of the class BFME
/// 2 uses, and whether it changed, is not established here. Search
/// reverse/functions.csv for the class name to see which of its BFME 2
/// functions are identified.
///
/// \see RingRenderObjClass::Get_Cost,
///      RenderObjClass::Get_Obj_Space_Bounding_Box


/// \fn float RingRenderObjClass::Get_Cost(void) const
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_ww3d_render_object_helpers
/// \brief Reports the ring's rendering cost to the level-of-detail manager:
///        its polygon count at the current LOD.
///
/// Part of the RenderObjClass LOD interface. It is a virtual function, and
/// no direct caller is identified. Zero Hour: the predictive LOD
/// optimiser weighs each object's Get_Cost against its Get_Value to decide
/// which objects to draw in more or less detail, and the ring's polygon count
/// comes from a table of costs per LOD level.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Calls the virtual Get_Num_Polys and returns the result as a float. Unlike
/// the base RenderObjClass::Get_Cost, it does not replace a polygon count of
/// zero with a tiny non-zero value.
///
/// \return The polygon count of the current LOD, as a float.
///
/// \warning Identity not settled by the bytes. The body is the BFME 1
///          donor's ringobj.cpp, placed by searching the executable for its
///          compiled bytes. Zero Hour's identical Get_Cost functions include
///          SphereRenderObjClass's, StreakLineClass's and
///          SegmentedLineClass's; all call the same virtual slot, so the body
///          alone does not show which class it belongs to. The name may
///          change.
///
/// \see RingRenderObjClass, \ref sub_w3d_rendering


/// \fn bool MeshModelClass::Needs_Vertex_Normals(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_ww3d_render_object_helpers
/// \brief Tells the mesh renderer whether a mesh's vertices need normals.
///
/// Called while a mesh model is prepared for drawing. Its identified callers
/// are DX8FVFCategoryContainer::Define_FVF, which adds a normal to the mesh's
/// vertex format when this returns true, and Vertex_Split_Table's
/// constructor, which enables N-patch tessellation only for meshes that need
/// normals (and allow N-patches) when the device supports it. Leaving normals
/// out of prelit meshes makes their vertex buffers smaller.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - A mesh with none of the prelit flags set always needs normals, because
///   it is lit in real time. Zero Hour's prelit flags mark vertex-lit and
///   lightmapped meshes, and BFME 2 tests the same flag bits.
/// - A prelit mesh needs them only if its current material description says
///   so (MeshMatDescClass::Do_Mappers_Need_Normals): when the device supports
///   N-patches and the N-patch level is above one, or when a vertex material
///   of any pass uses a texture mapper that needs normals (Zero Hour: the
///   environment mappers, among others).
///
/// This is Zero Hour's function unchanged.
///
/// \return true if the vertex buffer must include a normal per vertex.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Whether a mesh is prelit is decided by its W3D file (Zero Hour: the
/// prelit attributes of the mesh header), so a lightmapped or vertex-lit
/// mesh is drawn without normals unless its materials or the device need
/// them.
///
/// \see MeshModelClass::Compute_Ram_Size, \ref grp_w2_ww3d_core_helpers


/// \fn void WaterRenderObjClass::setGridTransform(const Matrix3D *transform)
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_ww3d_render_object_helpers
/// \brief Positions the gridded water surface by passing a transform to the
///        water's grid object.
///
/// Its identified caller is W3DTerrainVisual::setWaterTransform, the terrain
/// visual's matrix form, which forwards the transform when the terrain has a
/// water render object. Zero Hour: TerrainLogic raises or lowers gridded water
/// this way, by reading the water transform, changing its height and
/// setting it again. The BFME 2 caller of setWaterTransform is not
/// identified.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// If the water render object has a grid object, the transform pointer is
/// passed unchanged to a virtual function of that object; otherwise nothing
/// happens. The pointer itself is not checked here.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's and the BFME 1 reconstruction's version applies a
///       non-null transform to the water render object itself
///       (Set_Transform). BFME 2 delegates to the separate grid object
///       instead; what that object is, and what its function does with the
///       transform, is not established.
///
/// \param transform The new world transform of the water grid. Passed on
///                  without a null check.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// In BFME 2 the matrix form of the water-table transform
/// (W3DTerrainVisual::setWaterTransform) ends at the grid object, not at the
/// water render object. A replacement water renderer has to accept the
/// transform there, or change this function.
///
/// \see \ref sub_w3d_rendering


/// \fn void DLNodeClass<T>::Insert_Before(DLNodeClass<T> *n)
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_ww3d_render_object_helpers
/// \brief Links this node into a doubly linked list directly in front of an
///        existing node.
///
/// DLNodeClass and DLListClass, from dllist.h, are WW3D2's intrusive doubly
/// linked list: each node stores its successor, its predecessor and the list
/// it belongs to, and the list stores its head and tail. Retail has an
/// out-of-line copy of this member for SortingNodeStruct, the node type of
/// the sorting renderer's deferred transparent geometry. Its identified
/// caller is SortingRendererClass::Insert_VolumeParticle, and one other
/// sorting insertion routine calls it too. Zero Hour: the sorting renderer
/// keeps its nodes ordered by transformed depth; it walks the list from the
/// head and inserts the new node in front of the first node that is nearer
/// than it, or appends it at the tail.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Takes the list from n, links this node between n's predecessor and n,
/// and makes this node the list's head when n was the head. It does not
/// unlink this node from any list it is already in, and it never updates
/// the tail, which is correct because a node inserted in front of another is
/// never last. This is the template from Zero Hour's dllist.h, unchanged.
///
/// \param n The node to insert in front of. It must already be in a list;
///          null is not allowed.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Zero Hour: sorted insertion walks the list linearly, so the cost of
/// sorting transparent geometry grows with the square of the number of
/// sorted items in the worst case; the original source carries a note
/// suggesting a bucket sort instead.
///
/// \see \ref sub_w3d_rendering


/// \fn bool Render_Obj_Exists(const char *name)
/// \brief Reports whether an asset of the given name is registered with
///        BFME 2's asset registry.
///
/// The existence check of BFME 2's asset system, which replaces Zero Hour's
/// WW3DAssetManager (whose Render_Obj_Exists is a member that looks for a
/// prototype). The WorldBuilder build names its counterpart
/// AssetManager::HasAsset, in the assetmanager library. Callers use it to
/// avoid registering a name twice and to load optional assets only when
/// they exist. Its callers include Register_Aggregate_Prototype and several
/// other functions with the same registrar signature, FXShaderAsset::Register,
/// ModelSMConditionInfo::GetAssetList and RoadType::loadTexture.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Returns false for a null name or when the global asset registry has not
/// been created. Otherwise it returns AssetRegistry::Render_Obj_Exists_Impl,
/// which takes the registry's lock, converts the name to a lower-case name
/// key (false if the key is invalid) and looks the key up in the registry's
/// hash map. The WorldBuilder build also asserts that the name is not null;
/// retail just returns false.
///
/// \param name Asset name. Case does not matter. Null is allowed and gives
///             false.
/// \return true if the registry holds an asset under that name.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Only registered names are found. Names are registered by the asset
/// catalog's registrars, such as Register_Aggregate_Prototype, while the
/// asset index is read (\ref sub_w3d_rendering_modding). An optional
/// asset, such as a road texture's normal-map companion, is used only if its
/// name is registered; a missing one is skipped without any message.
///
/// \see Register_Aggregate_Prototype, \ref grp_w2_ww3d_core_helpers
